<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Banate of Jajce</h1><p class="st-meta">1464 CE – 1527 CE · capital Jajce</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Hungarian frontier province built around the Bosnian royal fortress of Jajce, which held back the Ottomans for more than sixty years after the fall of Bosnia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a> in 1463, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> counter-attacked and retook the fortress town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> after a siege in December 1463. In 1464 he organised the recovered strongholds of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> as a banate, a military province under a ban, to shield <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and southern Hungary.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The banate was essentially a chain of garrisoned castles along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliva">Pliva</a> valleys, financed by the Hungarian crown and commanded by bans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-szapolyai">Emeric Szapolyai</a>. It survived an Ottoman siege led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> himself in 1464 and later attacks, relieved in 1525 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krsto-frankopan">Krsto Frankopan</a>. Its Catholic population shrank as war emptied the countryside.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Hungarian defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a> in 1526, the crown could no longer supply its Bosnian outposts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jajce">Jajce</a> surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1527. The town became part of the Ottoman sanjak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, and the frontier moved north into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1464 CE – 1527 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.84%;width:1.25%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1464 CE – 1527 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jajce</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Matthias Corvinus, Emeric Szapolyai, Petar Keglević</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bosnia and Herzegovina</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jajce Banate</dd></dl></aside></div>

